  "strategy": "We are given a parent solution with n=100 samples achieving a bound of 0.381307. Our goal is to improve upon this by finding a step function on a higher-resolution grid that reduces the maximum overlap. \n\n## Approach and Rationale\nWe will use a two-stage strategy:\n1. Coarse-scale sampling: First attempt to find good solutions at moderate resolutions (n=200) using a candidate population that includes:\n   - Linear interpolation of the parent solution\n   - Random feasible starts\n   - Slightly perturbed versions of the parent solution\n2. High-res refinement: For each promising solution found at n=200, we will refine it by upsampling to n=400 while preserving feasibility and convexity.\n\nThe key innovation is combining coarse optimization over solvable dimensions (n=200) with targeted refinement to higher resolution (n=400). We do this with strict time management because the evaluation cost grows quadratically with grid size.\n\nThe algorithm:\n- Uses gradient-based optimization for better convergence\n- Projects candidates back to the feasible set after each step\n- Maintains \u03f5-constraint satisfaction throughout\n- Uses a novel \"clipping-reflection\" repair operator for log-concave distributions\n\n## Mathematical Specification\n**Representation**: Piecewise constant construction over [0,2] with n samples.\n\n**Objective**: Minimize constraints and the overlap integral  \nC5 = max_k \u27e8h, 1-h_shifted\u27e9 (dx factor not shown here)\n\n**Approximation**: Use soft-max proxy for interior points, exact computation for evaluation.\n\n**Feasibility constraints**:\n- \u222b h dx = 1\n- 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1\n\n## Implementation Procedure\n1. **Parent initialization**\n   - For given grid n, create symmetric representation to reduce dimension\n   - Include upsampled parental candidate\n   - Generate 3-5 random feasible candidates\n   - Create perturbations by uniform shift/clip on parent features\n\n2. **Two-stage optimization for each candidate**\n   Stage 1: \n   - Use simplex-constrained L-BFGS-B\n   - \u03b1=15 (regularized log-sum-exp)\n   - Allow up to 300 function evaluations\n Stage 2:\n   - Switch to COBYLA for constraint satisfaction\n   - \u03b1=25\n   - Limited to 200 evaluations\n\n3. **Repair/feasibility**\n   - Volume preservation as fallback\n   - For boundary violations: enforce with projection\n   - Clip aggressive deviations\n\n4. **Grid handling**\n   - n=200: medium resolution for exploration\n   - n=400: high resolution for exploitation\n   - Maintains consistency between resolutions\n\n## Search Choices and Variations\n- **Grid sizes**: [200, 400] (skipped n=100 as timely improvement)\n- **Diversity strategy**: parent, simplex, uniform\n- **Perturbation**: 50% max deviation, log-scale on flat distributions\n- **Time budgeting**: 80% for n=200, 60% for n=400\n- **Repair**: 3 global repair steps for unstable solutions\n\n## Compute Budget and Robustness\n- **n=200**: Dominant operation = correlation on 399 points\n- **n=400**: Dominant operation = correlation on 799 points\n- Time estimates: \n  - n=200: 450-600s\n  - n=400: 900-1500s \u2248 half budget\n- Termination: Early exit if progress stalls\n- Fallback: Return best seen from n=200\n\n## Validation and Return Contract\n1. For each candidate h:\n   - Check \u222b h dx=1\u00b11e-5\n   - Check domain bounds: max(|h_i-0.5|)\u22640.5\n   - Re-evaluate C5 exactly each time keeper.\n\n2. Return format: (h_values, c5_bound, n_points)\n\nThe key differentiator is the adaptive grid progression from medium to high resolution, combined with proper constraint handling.",
